<mxCell id="ffmpegWorker" value="FFmpeg Transcode&#10;Workers (Spot)&#10;4 Resolutions" style="ellipse;shape=cloud;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffcc80;strokeColor=#ef6c00;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1540" y="540" width="240" height="100" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
<mxCell id="ffmpegArrow" value="video.uploaded&#10;event consume" style="html=1;verticalAlign=bottom;endArrow=block;strokeWidth=2;strokeColor=#ef6c00;dashed=1;fontSize=10" edge="1" parent="1" source="systemBoundary" target="ffmpegWorker"><mxGeometry width="80" relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
<mxCell id="rulesBox" value="6 KEY DESIGN RULES FOR ONLINE LEARNING PLATFORM&#10;&#10;1. ASYNC VIDEO TRANSCODING NEVER SYNC (S3 → LAMBDA → KAFKA → SPOT WORKERS): Instructor uploads 2GB-5GB raw video via presigned S3 URL (direct upload bypasses server, resumable chunked). S3 PUT event triggers Lambda which publishes Kafka video.uploaded event. Video Processing Worker (Spot instances, 70% cheaper) consumes async: FFmpeg transcodes to 4 resolutions (1080p 5Mbps, 720p 2.5Mbps, 480p 1Mbps, 360p 500Kbps), packages into HLS 6-second segments with master.m3u8 playlist, extracts thumbnails, uploads to S3. Takes 10-15 minutes. WHY ASYNC: holding HTTP connection 15 min for sync transcode causes timeouts, wastes always-on server CPU, terrible UX. Instructor gets instant upload confirmation, email when ready.&#10;&#10;2. CDN MANDATORY FOR LESS THAN 2 SECOND VIDEO STARTUP: Raw S3 direct access = 200-500ms round-trip latency globally = fails startup target. CloudFront 200+ edge locations cache HLS segments near students (95%+ cache hit rate for popular courses). Startup breakdown: DNS 50ms + TLS 100ms + manifest download 200ms + first segment 500ms = 850ms total, well under 2 sec target. Segment cache TTL 30 days (immutable content), manifest cache TTL 1 hour. Adaptive bitrate: player measures bandwidth via first segment, selects resolution (10Mbps→1080p, 2.5Mbps→720p, 1Mbps→480p, less than 1Mbps→360p), switches seamlessly mid-stream on bandwidth changes.&#10;&#10;3. PROGRESS TRACKING WITH TWO POSITION FIELDS (RESUME VS COMPLETION): last_position_sec always stores latest position sent (where to resume playback). max_position_sec uses GREATEST(existing, new) so it never decreases even on rewind (farthest ever watched, used for completion check). Completion triggers when max_position_sec >= duration_sec × 0.95 (95% watched threshold). WHY TWO FIELDS: if student rewinds to relisten a concept then closes tab, storing only current position would un-complete their progress incorrectly. Client batches 3 heartbeats over 30 seconds before sending, reducing API calls 3x (1M students × 6 events/min = 6M calls/min becomes 2M calls/min). Kafka video.progress topic 100 partitions by user_id absorbs bursts, returns 200 OK immediately without blocking video playback.&#10;&#10;4. FIVE-LAYER ENROLLMENT IDEMPOTENCY (PREVENTS DOUBLE CHARGE/DOUBLE ENROLL): Layer 1: client-generated enrollmentRequestId passed to Stripe metadata, duplicate clicks return existing PaymentIntent (no second charge). Layer 2: Kafka consumer checks DB SELECT COUNT before INSERT enrollment, skips if already enrolled (handles duplicate webhook delivery). Layer 3: UNIQUE(user_id, course_id) constraint is the database-level safety net against race conditions. Layer 4: polling backup queries Stripe every 5 minutes for PENDING payments in case webhook is lost entirely. Layer 5: all paths converge to same idempotent outcome regardless of event ordering. CRITICAL: server never generates idempotency keys, always client-generated to survive network retries correctly.&#10;&#10;5. ACCESS TABLE SEPARATE FROM ENROLLMENTS TABLE (SCOPE VS HISTORY): Enrollment = permanent historical record of joining a course, never deleted even after refund. Access = keycard that determines if student can watch video RIGHT NOW, with scope_type (LIFETIME one-time purchase or SUBSCRIPTION rolling) and status (ACTIVE/REVOKED/EXPIRED). Permission Sync Service listens to Kafka events: payment.completed inserts ACTIVE access, refund.issued within 30-day window sets REVOKED, subscription.expired sets EXPIRED. Video Playback Service checks Access table (not just Enrollments) before generating CloudFront signed URL. WHY SEPARATE: refund revokes access without mutating enrollment history; subscription users can be enrolled in 50 courses with independent access windows; audits show both who ever enrolled and who can watch right now as independent answers.&#10;&#10;6. CAP THEOREM SPLIT (AP FOR STREAMING, CP FOR PAYMENTS): Video catalog browsing and streaming = AP (Availability over Consistency) — slightly stale course rating or view count is completely acceptable, video must always be watchable. Payment and enrollment = CP (Consistency over Availability) — during a network partition, reject enrollment writes rather than risk double-charging a student or enrolling without payment confirmation. This explicit tradeoff must be stated in interviews: never sacrifice payment consistency for streaming availability, and never sacrifice streaming availability chasing perfect consistency on view counts." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e3f2fd;strokeColor=#1976d2;strokeWidth=3;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="820" width="1780" height="300" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
<mxCell id="analogy" value="BEGINNER ANALOGY: Online Learning Platform as a University Campus with a Video Library&#10;&#10;Imagine a university with a giant video library and a campus security system. The MEDIA UPLOADER SERVICE is the loading dock where professors drop off raw lecture footage (raw 2GB video uploaded directly via presigned URL, bypassing the front desk entirely). Behind the loading dock, a PROCESSING LAB (FFmpeg Spot Workers) works overnight cutting the lecture into standardized reels at 4 quality levels (1080p/720p/480p/360p) so any classroom projector can play it smoothly regardless of its speed — this takes 10-15 minutes, so the professor gets a receipt immediately and a notification email later when the lecture is ready to view. The CDN is a chain of 200 local libraries worldwide, each holding cached copies of popular lectures so a student in Mumbai does not wait for a courier from the main US library (Origin S3) every time they hit play — 95% of requests are served from the nearest local library in under 50ms. The ENROLLMENT DESK checks two different records before letting you into a lecture hall: your STUDENT ID CARD (Enrollment — permanent proof you registered for the course, never revoked) and your CURRENT KEYCARD (Access — can be deactivated if you get a refund, or expires if your subscription lapses). A bookmark (last_position_sec) always shows exactly where you stopped reading, while a separate highlighter mark (max_position_sec) shows the farthest page you've ever reached — even if you flip back to reread a chapter, your highlighter mark never moves backward, which is how the system knows you actually finished the book (95% watched = complete). Course PAYMENT works like buying a ticket at the box office: your credit card is charged once with a unique ticket stub number (idempotency key) so pressing buy twice by accident never charges you twice — 5 separate security checks make sure of this. Finally, the CERTIFICATE PRINTER only runs once per completed course, stamping your name on a diploma and filing it in the permanent archive (S3) — never reprinted twice even if the printer's control system restarts mid-job." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff9c4;strokeColor=#f57f17;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="1140" width="1780" height="180" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
<mxCell id="scaleNote" value="SCALE: 100K+ courses, millions of enrolled students globally, billions of HLS segments in S3, petabytes of content, millions of video requests/day, thousands of enrollments/hour, video startup less than 2 sec, API latency less than 200ms course listing less than 100ms enrollment check, 99.9% uptime, 11 nines durability S3, CAP AP for streaming CP for payments" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=#7b1fa2;fillColor=#f3e5f5;align=center;fontSize=11;rounded=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="320" y="420" width="720" height="50" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
</root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>
